    "js": {
      "name": "JavaScript",
      "sdks": {
        "azure-ai-inference": {
          "name": "Azure AI Inference SDK",
          "content": "\n\n\n\nBelow are example code snippets for a few use cases. For additional information about Azure AI Inference SDK, see full [documentation](https://aka.ms/azsdk/azure-ai-inference/js/reference) and [samples](https://aka.ms/azsdk/azure-ai-inference/js/samples).\n\n\n## 1. Create a personal access token\n\n\nYou'll need to **[create a token](https://github.com/settings/tokens?type=beta)** to enable free API access.\n\nYou **do not need to give any permissions** to the token. Note that the token will be sent to a Microsoft service.\n\nTo use the code snippets below, create an environment variable to set your token as the key for the client code.\n\nIf you're using **bash**:\n\n```bash\nexport GITHUB_TOKEN=\"<your-github-token-goes-here>\"\n```\n\nIf you're in **powershell**:\n\n```powershell\n$Env:GITHUB_TOKEN=\"<your-github-token-goes-here>\"\n```\n\nIf you're using Windows command prompt:\n\n```\nset GITHUB_TOKEN=<your-github-token-goes-here>\n```\n\n\n\n## 2. Install dependencies\n\n1. Install [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/).\n\n2. Copy the following lines of text and save them as a file `package.json` inside your folder.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"@azure-rest/ai-inference\": \"latest\",\n    \"@azure/core-auth\": \"latest\",\n    \"@azure/core-sse\": \"latest\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n> Note: `@azure/core-sse` is only needed when you stream the chat completions response.\n\n3. Open a terminal window in this folder and run `npm install`.\n\n4. For each of the code snippets below, copy the content into a file `sample.js` and run with `node sample.js`.\n\n\n\n## 3. Run a basic code sample\n\nThis sample demonstrates a basic call to the chat completion API. It is leveraging the GitHub AI model inference endpoint and your GitHub token. The call is synchronous.\n\n```js\nimport ModelClient from \"@azure-rest/ai-inference\";\nimport { AzureKeyCredential } from \"@azure/core-auth\";\n\nconst token = process.env[\"GITHUB_TOKEN\"];\nconst endpoint = \"https://models.inference.ai.azure.com\";\nconst modelName = \"Cohere-command-r-plus\";\n\nexport async function main() {\n\n  const client = new ModelClient(\n    endpoint,\n    new AzureKeyCredential(token),\n  );\n\n  const response = await client.path(\"/chat/completions\").post({\n    body: {\n      messages: [\n        { role:\"system\", content: \"You are a helpful assistant.\" },\n        { role:\"user\", content: \"What is the capital of France?\" }\n      ],\n      temperature: 1.0,\n      top_p: 1.0,\n      max_tokens: 1000,\n      model: modelName\n    }\n  });\n\n  if (response.status !== \"200\") {\n    throw response.body.error;\n  }\n  console.log(response.body.choices[0].message.content);\n}\n\nmain().catch((err) => {\n  console.error(\"The sample encountered an error:\", err);\n});\n```\n\n\n\n## 4. Explore more samples\n\n\n\n\n### Run a multi-turn conversation\n\nThis sample demonstrates a multi-turn conversation with the chat completion API. When using the model for a chat application, you'll need to manage the history of that conversation and send the latest messages to the model.\n\n```js\nimport ModelClient from \"@azure-rest/ai-inference\";\nimport { AzureKeyCredential } from \"@azure/core-auth\";\n\nconst token = process.env[\"GITHUB_TOKEN\"];\nconst endpoint = \"https://models.inference.ai.azure.com\";\nconst modelName = \"Cohere-command-r-plus\";\n\nexport async function main() {\n\n  const client = new ModelClient(\n    endpoint,\n    new AzureKeyCredential(token),\n  );\n\n  const response = await client.path(\"/chat/completions\").post({\n    body: {\n      messages: [\n        { role: \"system\", content: \"You are a helpful assistant.\" },\n        { role: \"user\", content: \"What is the capital of France?\" },\n        { role: \"assistant\", content: \"The capital of France is Paris.\" },\n        { role: \"user\", content: \"What about Spain?\" },\n      ],\n      model: modelName,\n    }\n  });\n\n  if (response.status !== \"200\") {\n    throw response.body.error;\n  }\n\n  for (const choice of response.body.choices) {\n    console.log(choice.message.content);\n  }\n}\n\nmain().catch((err) => {\n  console.error(\"The sample encountered an error:\", err);\n});\n```\n\n### Stream the output\n\nFor a better user experience, you will want to stream the response of the model so that the first token shows up early and you avoid waiting for long responses.\n\n```js\nimport ModelClient from \"@azure-rest/ai-inference\";\nimport { AzureKeyCredential } from \"@azure/core-auth\";\nimport { createSseStream } from \"@azure/core-sse\";\n\nconst token = process.env[\"GITHUB_TOKEN\"];\nconst endpoint = \"https://models.inference.ai.azure.com\";\nconst modelName = \"Cohere-command-r-plus\";\n\nexport async function main() {\n\n  const client = new ModelClient(endpoint, new AzureKeyCredential(token));\n\n  const response = await client.path(\"/chat/completions\").post({\n    body: {\n      messages: [\n        { role: \"system\", content: \"You are a helpful assistant.\" },\n        { role: \"user\", content: \"Give me 5 good reasons why I should exercise every day.\" },\n      ],\n      model: modelName,\n      stream: true\n    }\n  }).asNodeStream();\n\n  const stream = response.body;\n  if (!stream) {\n    throw new Error(\"The response stream is undefined\");\n  }\n\n  if (response.status !== \"200\") {\n    stream.destroy();\n    throw new Error(`Failed to get chat completions, http operation failed with ${response.status} code`);\n  }\n\n  const sseStream = createSseStream(stream);\n\n  for await (const event of sseStream) {\n    if (event.data === \"[DONE]\") {\n      return;\n    }\n    for (const choice of (JSON.parse(event.data)).choices) {\n        process.stdout.write(choice.delta?.content ?? ``);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nmain().catch((err) => {\n  console.error(\"The sample encountered an error:\", err);\n});\n```\n\n### Identify and invoke tools\n\nA language model can be given a set of tools it can invoke, for running specific actions depending on the context of the conversation. This sample demonstrates how to define a function tool and how to act on a request from the model to invoke it.\n\n```js\nimport ModelClient from \"@azure-rest/ai-inference\";\nimport { AzureKeyCredential } from \"@azure/core-auth\";\n\nconst token = process.env[\"GITHUB_TOKEN\"];\nconst endpoint = \"https://models.inference.ai.azure.com\";\nconst modelName = \"Cohere-command-r-plus\";\n\nfunction getFlightInfo({originCity, destinationCity}){\n  if (originCity === \"Seattle\" && destinationCity === \"Miami\"){\n    return JSON.stringify({\n      airline: \"Delta\",\n      flight_number: \"DL123\",\n      flight_date: \"May 7th, 2024\",\n      flight_time: \"10:00AM\"\n    });\n  }\n  return JSON.stringify({error: \"No flights found between the cities\"});\n}\n\nconst namesToFunctions = {\n  getFlightInfo: (data) =>\n  getFlightInfo(data),\n};\n\nexport async function main() {\n  \n  const tool = {\n    \"type\": \"function\",\n    \"function\": {\n      name: \"getFlightInfo\",\n      description: \"Returns information about the next flight between two cities.\" +\n               \"This includes the name of the airline, flight number and the date and time\" +\n               \"of the next flight\",\n      parameters: {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"originCity\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"The name of the city where the flight originates\",\n          },\n          \"destinationCity\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\", \n            \"description\": \"The flight destination city\",\n          },\n        },\n        \"required\": [\n          \"originCity\",\n          \"destinationCity\"\n        ],\n      }\n    }\n  };\n\n  const client = new ModelClient(endpoint, new AzureKeyCredential(token));\n\n  let messages=[\n    {role: \"system\", content: \"You an assistant that helps users find flight information.\"},\n    {role: \"user\", content: \"I'm interested in going to Miami. What is the next flight there from Seattle?\"},\n  ];\n\n  let response = await client.path(\"/chat/completions\").post({\n      body: {\n      messages: messages,\n    tools: [tool],\n      model: modelName\n    }\n  });\n  if (response.status !== \"200\") {\n    throw response.body.error;\n  }\n  \n  // We expect the model to ask for a tool call\n  if (response.body.choices[0].finish_reason === \"tool_calls\"){\n\n    // Append the model response to the chat history\n    messages.push(response.body.choices[0].message);\n    \n    // We expect a single tool call\n    if (response.body.choices[0].message && response.body.choices[0].message.tool_calls.length === 1){\n\n      const toolCall = response.body.choices[0].message.tool_calls[0];\n      // We expect the tool to be a function call\n      if (toolCall.type === \"function\"){\n        // Parse the function call arguments and call the function\n        const functionArgs = JSON.parse(toolCall.function.arguments);\n        console.log(`Calling function \\`${toolCall.function.name}\\` with arguments ${toolCall.function.arguments}`);\n        const callableFunc = namesToFunctions[toolCall.function.name];\n        const functionReturn = callableFunc(functionArgs);\n        console.log(`Function returned = ${functionReturn}`);\n\n        // Append the function call result fo the chat history\n        messages.push(\n          {\n            \"tool_call_id\": toolCall.id,\n            \"role\": \"tool\",\n            \"name\": toolCall.function.name,\n            \"content\": functionReturn,\n          }\n        )\n\n        response = await client.path(\"/chat/completions\").post({\n          body: {\n            messages: messages,\n            tools: [tool],\n            model: modelName\n          }\n        });\n        if (response.status !== \"200\") {\n          throw response.body.error;\n        }\n        console.log(`Model response = ${response.body.choices[0].message.content}`);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nmain().catch((err) => {\n  console.error(\"The sample encountered an error:\", err);\n});\n```\n\n\n\n\n## 5. Going beyond rate limits\n\nThe rate limits for the playground and free API usage are intended to help you experiment with models and prototype your AI application. For use beyond those limits, and to bring your application to scale, you must provision resources from an Azure account, and authenticate from there instead of your GitHub personal access token. You don't need to change anything else in your code. Use this link to [discover how to go beyond the free tier limits in Azure AI](https://ai.azure.com/github?modelName=Cohere-command-r-plus).",
          "code_sample": "/*\r\nRun this model in Javascript\r\n\r\n> npm install @azure-rest/ai-inference @azure/core-auth @azure/core-sse\r\n*/\r\nimport ModelClient from \"@azure-rest/ai-inference\";\r\nimport { AzureKeyCredential } from \"@azure/core-auth\";\r\n\r\n// To authenticate with the model you will need to generate a personal access token (PAT) in your GitHub settings. \r\n// Create your PAT token by following instructions here: https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/managing-your-personal-access-tokens\r\nconst token = process.env[\"GITHUB_TOKEN\"];\r\n\r\nexport async function main() {\r\n    const client = new ModelClient(\r\n        \"{model_endpoint}\",\r\n        new AzureKeyCredential(token)\r\n    );\r\n\r\n    const response = await client.path(\"/chat/completions\").post({\r\n        body: {\r\n            messages: [\r\n                { role: \"system\", content: \"{system_message}\" },\r\n                { role: \"user\", content: \"Can you explain the basics of machine learning?\" }\r\n            ],\r\n            model: \"{model_name}\",\r\n            temperature: {temperature},\r\n            max_tokens: {max_tokens},\r\n            top_p: {top_p}\r\n        }\r\n    });\r\n\r\n    if (response.status !== \"200\") {\r\n        throw response.body.error;\r\n    }\r\n    console.log(response.body.choices[0].message.content);\r\n}\r\n\r\nmain().catch((err) => {\r\n    console.error(\"The sample encountered an error:\", err);\r\n});\r\n"
        },
        "cohereai": {
          "name": "Cohere AI",
          "content": "\n\n\n\n\n\n## 1. Create a personal access token\n\n\nYou'll need to **[create a token](https://github.com/settings/tokens?type=beta)** to enable free API access.\n\nYou **do not need to give any permissions** to the token. Note that the token will be sent to a Microsoft service.\n\nTo use the code snippets below, create an environment variable to set your token as the key for the client code.\n\nIf you're using **bash**:\n\n```bash\nexport GITHUB_TOKEN=\"<your-github-token-goes-here>\"\n```\n\nIf you're in **powershell**:\n\n```powershell\n$Env:GITHUB_TOKEN=\"<your-github-token-goes-here>\"\n```\n\nIf you're using Windows command prompt:\n\n```\nset GITHUB_TOKEN=<your-github-token-goes-here>\n```\n\n\n\n## 2. Install dependencies\n\n1. Install [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/).\n\n2. Copy the following lines of text and save them as a file `package.json` inside your folder.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"cohere-ai\": \"latest\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n3. Open a terminal window in this folder and run `npm install`.\n\n4. For each of the code snippets below, copy the content into a file `sample.js` and run with `node sample.js`.\n\n\n\n## 3. Run a basic code sample\n\nThis sample demonstrates a basic call to the chat completion API. It is leveraging the GitHub AI model inference endpoint and your GitHub token. The call is synchronous.\n\n```js\nimport { CohereClient } from \"cohere-ai\";\n\nconst token = process.env[\"GITHUB_TOKEN\"];\nconst endpoint = \"https://models.inference.ai.azure.com\";\nconst modelName = \"Cohere-command-r-plus\";\n\nexport async function main() {\n\n  const client = new CohereClient({\n    environment: endpoint,\n    token: token,\n  });\n\n  const response = await client.chat({\n    model: modelName,\n    preamble: \"You are a helpful assistant.\",\n    message: \"What is the capital of France?\",\n    temperature: 1.0,\n    maxTokens: 1000,\n    p: 1.0\n  });\n\n  console.log(response.text)\n}\n\nmain().catch((err) => {\n  console.error(\"The sample encountered an error:\", err);\n});\n```\n\n\n\n## 4. Explore more samples\n\n\n\n\n### Identify and invoke tools\n\nA language model can be given a set of tools it can invoke, for running specific actions depending on the context of the conversation. This sample demonstrates how to define a function tool and how to act on a request from the model to invoke it.\n\n```js\nimport { Cohere, CohereClient } from \"cohere-ai\";\n\nconst token = process.env[\"GITHUB_TOKEN\"];\nconst endpoint = \"https://models.inference.ai.azure.com\";\nconst modelName = \"Cohere-command-r-plus\";\n\nfunction getFlightInfo({originCity, destinationCity}){\n  if (originCity === \"Seattle\" && destinationCity === \"Miami\"){\n    return JSON.stringify({\n      airline: \"Delta\",\n      flight_number: \"DL123\",\n      flight_date: \"May 7th, 2024\",\n      flight_time: \"10:00AM\"\n    });\n  }\n  return JSON.stringify({error: \"No flights found between the cities\"});\n}\n\nconst namesToFunctions = {\n  getFlightInfo: (data) =>\n  getFlightInfo(data),\n};\n\nexport async function main() {\n\n  const tool = {\n    name: \"getFlightInfo\",\n    description: \"Returns information about the next flight between two cities. \" +\n                 \"This includes the name of the airline, flight number \" +\n                 \"and the date and time of the next flight\",\n    parameterDefinitions: {\n      originCity: {\n        description: \"The name of the city where the flight originates\",\n        type: \"string\",\n        required: true\n      },\n        destinationCity: {\n        description: \"The flight destination city\",\n        type: \"string\",\n        required: true\n      }\n    }\n  };\n  \n  const client = new CohereClient({\n    environment: endpoint,\n    token: token,\n  });\n\n  let messages = [\n    {role: \"SYSTEM\", message: \"You an assistant that helps users find flight information.\"},\n    {role: \"USER\", message: \"I'm interested in going to Miami. What is the next flight there from Seattle?\"},\n  ]\n\n  let response = await client.chat({\n    model: modelName,\n  tools: [tool],\n    preamble: messages[0].message,\n    message: messages[1].message\n  });\n  \n  // We expect the model to ask for a tool call\n  if (response.finishReason === \"COMPLETE\"){\n    // We expect a single tool call\n    if (response.toolCalls && response.toolCalls.length == 1){\n      messages.push(\n        {\n          role: 'CHATBOT',\n          message: JSON.stringify(response.toolCalls[0].parameters),\n          toolCall: response.toolCalls[0]\n        }\n      );\n      \n      const toolCall = response.toolCalls[0];\n      // Parse the function call arguments and call the function\n      const functionArgs = toolCall.parameters;\n      console.log(`Calling function \\`${toolCall.name}\\` with arguments ${JSON.stringify(functionArgs)}`);\n      const callableFunc = namesToFunctions[toolCall.name];\n      const functionReturn = callableFunc(functionArgs);\n      console.log(`Function returned = ${functionReturn}`);\n\n      // Get another response from the model\n      response = await client.chat({\n        message: \"\",\n        chat_history: messages,\n        tools: [tool],\n        tool_results: [\n          {\n            call: toolCall,\n            outputs: [JSON.parse(functionReturn)]\n          }\n        ],\n        model: modelName,\n      });\n      console.log(`Model response = ${response.text}`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nmain().catch((err) => {\n  console.error(\"The sample encountered an error:\", err);\n});\n```\n\n### Run a multi-turn conversation\n\nThis sample demonstrates a multi-turn conversation with the chat completion API. When using the model for a chat application, you'll need to manage the history of that conversation and send the latest messages to the model.\n\n```js\nimport { CohereClient } from \"cohere-ai\";\n\nconst token = process.env[\"GITHUB_TOKEN\"];\nconst endpoint = \"https://models.inference.ai.azure.com\";\nconst modelName = \"Cohere-command-r-plus\";\n\nexport async function main() {\n\n  const client = new CohereClient({\n    environment: endpoint,\n    token: token,\n  });\n\n  const response = await client.chat({\n  model: modelName,\n  chatHistory: [\n    {role: \"SYSTEM\", message: \"You are a helpful assistant.\"},\n    {role: \"USER\", message: \"What is the capital of France?\"},\n    {role: \"CHATBOT\", message: \"The capital of France is Paris.\"},\n  ],\n    message: \"What about Spain?\"\n  });\n\n  console.log(response.text)\n}\n\nmain().catch((err) => {\n  console.error(\"The sample encountered an error:\", err);\n});\n```\n\n### Stream the output\n\nFor a better user experience, you will want to stream the response of the model so that the first token shows up early and you avoid waiting for long responses.\n\n```js\nimport { CohereClient } from \"cohere-ai\";\n\nconst token = process.env[\"GITHUB_TOKEN\"];\nconst endpoint = \"https://models.inference.ai.azure.com\";\nconst modelName = \"Cohere-command-r-plus\";\n\nexport async function main() {\n\n  const client = new CohereClient({\n    environment: endpoint,\n    token: token,\n  });\n\n  const response = await client.chatStream({\n    model: modelName,\n    preamble: \"You are a helpful assistant.\",\n    message: \"Give me 5 good reasons why I should exercise every day.\"\n  });\n\n  for await (const chunk of response) {\n    if (chunk.eventType  === \"text-generation\") {\n      const streamText = chunk.text;\n      process.stdout.write(streamText);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nmain().catch((err) => {\n  console.error(\"The sample encountered an error:\", err);\n});\n```\n\n\n\n\n## 5. Going beyond rate limits\n\nThe rate limits for the playground and free API usage are intended to help you experiment with models and prototype your AI application. For use beyond those limits, and to bring your application to scale, you must provision resources from an Azure account, and authenticate from there instead of your GitHub personal access token. You don't need to change anything else in your code. Use this link to [discover how to go beyond the free tier limits in Azure AI](https://ai.azure.com/github?modelName=Cohere-command-r-plus).",
          "code_sample": "/*\r\nRun this model in Javascript\r\n\r\n> npm install cohere-ai\r\n*/\r\nimport { CohereClient } from \"cohere-ai\";\r\n\r\n// To authenticate with the model you will need to generate a personal access token (PAT) in your GitHub settings. \r\n// Create your PAT token by following instructions here: https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/managing-your-personal-access-tokens\r\nconst token = process.env[\"GITHUB_TOKEN\"];\r\n\r\nexport async function main() {\r\n\r\n  const client = new CohereClient({\r\n    environment: \"https://models.inference.ai.azure.com\",\r\n    token: token,\r\n  });\r\n\r\n  const response = await client.chat({\r\n    model: \"cohere-command-r-plus\",\r\n    preamble: \"{system_message}\",\r\n    message: \"What is the capital of France?\",\r\n    temperature: {temperature},\r\n    maxTokens: {max_tokens},\r\n    p: {top_p}\r\n  });\r\n\r\n  console.log(response.text)\r\n}\r\n\r\nmain().catch((err) => {\r\n  console.error(\"The sample encountered an error:\", err);\r\n});\r\n"
